<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otto Truchsess von Waldburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1514–1573</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otto Truchsess von Waldburg was a German cardinal and prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> from 1543 to 1573, from the Swabian family of the Truchsess von Waldburg. A leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-dillingen">University of Dillingen</a> in 1551 and later entrusted it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, making the prince-bishopric a Catholic stronghold.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otto-truchsess-von-waldburg.jpg" alt="Otto Truchsess von Waldburg" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lambert Sustris · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otto_von_Waldburg_by_Sustris.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
